<Page path="concepts/catalogs">
<section id="why">
  <h2 id="why"><a href="#why">What a catalog is for</a></h2>
  <p>A multiverse describes one set of repositories. A <strong>catalog</strong> answers a different question: which multiverses exist, and which belong on this machine. It is only an index, and holds pointers, so each definition stays with the repositories it describes.</p>
  <p>Catalogs nest. A personal catalog can link one a team keeps in its own repository, which can link another; each layer is edited by whoever owns that repository, and a machine starts only the multiverses it wants.</p>
  <p>You do not need one: <code>multiversectl start acme/multiverse</code> works with no catalog at all, and a repository can hold several multiverses without any catalog listing them. The catalog is for keeping your own index of what you work on.</p>
</section>

<section id="kinds">
  <h2 id="kinds"><a href="#kinds">What a catalog lists</a></h2>
  <p>An entry is a multiverse or another catalog. They are two lists because the file has to say which is which: drawing the tree would otherwise mean cloning every entry to find out. Everything else treats them alike, so <code>catalog add</code> and <code>catalog rm</code> take either.</p>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="k">apiVersion</span>: multiverse/v1alpha1
<span class="k">kind</span>: Catalog
<span class="k">metadata</span>:
  <span class="k">name</span>: me
<span class="k">spec</span>:
  <span class="k">catalogs</span>:                         <span class="c"># descend into another Catalog</span>
    - <span class="k">name</span>: acme
      <span class="k">source</span>: &#123;<span class="k">repo</span>: git@example.test:acme/multiverse-index.git, <span class="k">ref</span>: main&#125;
    - <span class="k">name</span>: personal                <span class="c"># no repo: a file in this same repository</span>
      <span class="k">source</span>: &#123;<span class="k">path</span>: catalogs/personal.yaml&#125;
  <span class="k">multiverses</span>:                     <span class="c"># a Multiverse, a leaf</span>
    - <span class="k">name</span>: notes
      <span class="k">source</span>: &#123;<span class="k">repo</span>: git@example.test:me/notes-multiverse.git&#125;</pre></Terminal>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>source.repo</code></td><td>A git URL. Leave it out to mean the repository the entry is written in.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>source.ref</code></td><td>Branch, tag or commit. Needs <code>repo</code>. A tag or commit makes that layer read-only.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>source.path</code></td><td>Relative to the repository root, and cannot leave it. Defaults to <code>catalog.yaml</code> or <code>multiverse.yaml</code>; a second multiverse in the same repository is <code>multiverse/&lt;name&gt;.yaml</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>An entry is addressed by the names leading to it, such as <code>acme/backend/billing</code>; names are unique among siblings. The schema is <code>schema/catalog.v1alpha1.yaml</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="places">
  <h2 id="places"><a href="#places">Where a node lives</a></h2>
  <p>A repository is written as a URL, which says how it was typed rather than where it is, so everywhere one is shown the platform comes first:</p>
  <div class="tw"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">You see</th><th scope="col">It is</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-l="You see"><code>github:acme/api</code></td><td data-l="It is">a repository on GitHub, however its URL was written</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-l="You see"><code>gitlab:acme/team/api</code></td><td data-l="It is">a repository on GitLab, subgroups included</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-l="You see"><code>git:git.example.test/acme/api</code></td><td data-l="It is">a repository on any other host, with the host named</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-l="You see"><code>file:~/code/index/catalog.yaml</code></td><td data-l="It is">a file or directory on this machine</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>A branch, tag or commit follows as <code>@ref</code>, and self-hosted servers keep their host: <code>gitlab:gitlab.example.test/acme/api</code>. The tree says what each node is, whether each multiverse is installed, how many are installed under each catalog, and the file inside a repository when two catalogs share one:</p>
  <Terminal><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl catalog
index     <span class="c">root</span>       github:me/index                              1/2 installed
├─ personal  <span class="c">catalog</span>    github:me/index <span class="c">catalogs/personal.yaml</span>  0/1 installed
│  └─ notes     <span class="c">multiverse</span> file:~/notes/multiverse.yaml           ○ not installed
└─ acme      <span class="c">catalog</span>    gitlab:acme/index                            1/1 installed
   └─ billing  <span class="c">multiverse</span> gitlab:acme/billing                        ✓ installed as billing</pre></Terminal>
  <p>The web page draws the same, with each platform's mark and a badge for <em>root</em>, <em>catalog</em> or <em>multiverse</em>.</p>
</section>

<section id="root">
  <h2 id="root"><a href="#root">The root, and how a new machine finds it</a></h2>
  <p>Every machine starts from one catalog, its <em>root</em>. It is an ordinary <code>Catalog</code>; what makes it the root is that this machine points at it, and that <code>init</code> tags its repository with the topic <code>multiversectl-catalog</code>.</p>
  <p>On a hosting service <code>init</code> sets that topic, and on a machine with nothing it searches your repositories and organizations for it without cloning, so a new machine finds its index by itself.</p>
</section>

<section id="split">
  <h2 id="split"><a href="#split">Three separate things</a></h2>
  <ul class="t">
    <li><strong>The catalog</strong> is shared and versioned: what exists. Pointers, never definitions.</li>
    <li><strong>The multiverse list</strong> is per machine, in <code>~/.config/multiversectl/config.yaml</code>: what is started here, and where each came from.</li>
    <li><strong>Universes</strong> act on one multiverse and never read a catalog.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Only <code>start</code> connects the first two, and it only reads. <code>catalog add</code>, <code>new</code> and <code>rm</code> are what edit a catalog.</p>
</section>

<section id="names">
  <h2 id="names"><a href="#names">Names</a></h2>
  <p>A multiverse is installed under its own <code>metadata.name</code>, <code>billing</code>, in <code>~/multiverse/billing</code>. Names are unique on a machine: a second multiverse with the same name is refused, naming the file that defines it, so rename one of them there. Commands that act on one take <code>-m billing</code>; without it, the one whose folder you are in, else the only one installed. Anywhere a name is accepted, so is the catalog path it was installed from: <code>acme/backend/billing</code>.</p>
  <p>Each installed multiverse records where it came from, which is how <code>status</code> and <code>catalog update</code> know it has drifted.</p>
</section>

<section id="failure">
  <h2 id="failure"><a href="#failure">When a layer cannot be read</a></h2>
  <p>A layer you have no access to, or with a bad file, shows as <em>unavailable</em> with the reason, and the rest of the tree still resolves. Cycles and chains deeper than eight levels are cut off the same way.</p>
</section>

<section id="proxy">
  <h2 id="proxy"><a href="#proxy">Several multiverses, one proxy</a></h2>
  <p>A machine has one proxy, and starting any multiverse rewrites it from that multiverse's manifest. Sharing its network and the default <code>web</code> port is normal; wanting a different entrypoint, network or bind address is not, because starting the second multiverse takes the proxy from the first. <code>start</code> and <code>doctor</code> name those differences, by multiverse.</p>
</section>
</Page>
